// VecResizeBounded(values, count:s64, maximum_capacity:s64) -> bool has
// VecResize's initialized-length semantics and caps the backing element
// capacity. Negative sizes, count above the ceiling or existing capacity
// above the ceiling fail unchanged; shrinking never silently frees capacity.
// Bounds logical backing capacity, not allocator/runtime overhead or RSS.
// VecReserve(values, capacity: s64) -> bool reserves at least that total
// capacity without changing count. VecResize(values, count: s64) -> bool
// adjusts initialized length, giving new elements their language defaults
// and dropping removed owned elements. Both retain capacity on shrink/no-op,
// reject negative/overflow sizes, and leave storage unchanged on failure.
// Pass mutable storage, not a pointer; live borrowed views forbid mutation.
// Owned growable storage. Operations on Vec values are compiler primitives;
// the data pointer is private to those operations.
Vec :: struct($T: Type) {
    data: *T;
    count: s64;
    capacity: s64;
}

// Owned UTF-8 text. It moves and drops like the Vec it holds, so its bytes
// are freed when its owner goes away. TextView(text) borrows it as a string
// while the Text stays in place, and a Text passed where a string is
// expected lends the same view. The view cannot outlive the Text, and the
// Text cannot move or change while a view is live.
Text :: struct {
    bytes: Vec(u8);
}

// Copy borrowed text into an owned Text; empty when it cannot be allocated.
TextCopy :: (value: string) -> Text {
    text: Text
    if !BuilderAppend(text.bytes, value) { return .{} }
    return text
}
